      "text": "To solve the problem, we need to find the maximum possible area of an equilateral triangle that fits entirely within a rectangle \\(ABCD\\) with side lengths 10 and 11. The height of an equilateral triangle with side length \\(s\\) is given by \\(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} s\\). For the triangle to fit within the rectangle, the height of the triangle must be less than or equal to 11. The side length \\(s\\) of the triangle must also be less than or equal to 10 because the longest possible side length of the triangle is less than or equal to the shorter side of the rectangle.\n\nWe need to find the maximum possible side length \\(s\\) of the equilateral triangle such that the height is less than or equal to 11. The height of the triangle is \\(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} s = 11\\). Solving for \\(s\\), we get:\n\\[\n\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} s = 11 \\implies s = \\frac{11 \\cdot 2}{\\sqrt{3}} = \\frac{22}{\\sqrt{3}} = \\frac{22 \\sqrt{3}}{3}\n\\]\nThe area \\(A\\) of an equilateral triangle with side length \\(s\\) is given by:\n\\[\nA = \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4} s^2\n\\]\nSubstituting \\(s = \\frac{22 \\sqrt{3}}{3}\\) into the area formula, we get:\n\\[\nA = \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4} \\left( \\frac{22 \\sqrt{3}}{3} \\right)^2 = \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4} \\cdot \\frac{484 \\cdot 3}{9} = \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4} \\cdot \\frac{1452}{9} = \\frac{\\sqrt{3} \\cdot 1452}{36} = \\frac{1452 \\sqrt{3}}{36} = \\frac{121 \\sqrt{3}}{3}\n\\]\nTherefore, the maximum possible area of the equilateral triangle is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\frac{121\\sqrt{3}}{3}}\n\\]",
