      "sample_index": 2,
      "text": "To find the maximum possible area of an equilateral triangle that can be inscribed in a rectangle with side lengths 10 and 11, we need to consider the different possible positions of the triangle within the rectangle. The key is to determine the largest equilateral triangle that can fit in such a way that all its vertices lie within the rectangle.\n\nThe maximum possible side length of the equilateral triangle will be the distance between two of its vertices that are both inside the rectangle. We can consider two cases: placing the triangle such that one of its sides is parallel to one of the sides of the rectangle and placing it such that one of its sides is at an angle to the sides of the rectangle.\n\n**Case 1: The equilateral triangle is parallel to the sides of the rectangle.**\n\nIn this case, the side length of the equilateral triangle will be the distance between the two parallel sides of the rectangle. This distance is the same as the side length of the equilateral triangle. Let's denote the side length of the equilateral triangle by \\( s \\). The height \\( h \\) of an equilateral triangle with side length \\( s \\) is given by the formula:\n\n\\[ h = \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} s \\]\n\nSince the height of the equilateral triangle must be less than or equal to the height of the rectangle, we have:\n\n\\[ \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} s \\leq 10 \\]\n\nSolving for \\( s \\), we get:\n\n\\[ s \\leq \\frac{20}{\\sqrt{3}} = \\frac{20 \\sqrt{3}}{3} \\]\n\nThe area \\( A \\) of an equilateral triangle with side length \\( s \\) is given by:\n\n\\[ A = \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4} s^2 \\]\n\nSubstituting \\( s = \\frac{20 \\sqrt{3}}{3} \\) into the area formula, we get:\n\n\\[ A = \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4} \\left( \\frac{20 \\sqrt{3}}{3} \\right)^2 = \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4} \\cdot \\frac{400 \\cdot 3}{9} = \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4} \\cdot \\frac{1200}{9} = \\frac{300 \\sqrt{3}}{9} = \\frac{100 \\sqrt{3}}{3} \\]\n\n**Case 2: The equilateral triangle is rotated at an angle.**\n\nIn this case, the side length of the equilateral triangle will be the maximum possible, which we have already found to be \\( \\frac{20 \\sqrt{3}}{3} \\). However, to verify that this is indeed the maximum, we can consider the maximum height possible for the triangle.\n\nThe height of the equilateral triangle must be less than or equal to the diagonal of the rectangle, which is the maximum distance from one side to the opposite side. The diagonal \\( d \\) of the rectangle is given by:\n\n\\[ d = \\sqrt{10^2 + 11^2} = \\sqrt{100 + 121} = \\sqrt{221} \\]\n\nFor the triangle to fit, the maximum height \\( h \\) must be less than or equal to \\( \\sqrt{221} \\). The maximum side length \\( s \\) of the equilateral triangle will then be the distance between the two parallel sides of the rectangle, which is \\( \\frac{2 \\cdot 11}{\\sqrt{3}} = \\frac{22}{\\sqrt{3}} = \\frac{22 \\sqrt{3}}{3} \\).\n\nThe area \\( A \\) of an equilateral triangle with side length \\( s \\) is given by:\n\n\\[ A = \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4} \\left( \\frac{22 \\sqrt{3}}{3} \\right)^2 = \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4} \\cdot \\frac{484 \\cdot 3}{9} = \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4} \\cdot \\frac{1452}{9} = \\frac{1452 \\sqrt{3}}{36} = \\frac{113 \\sqrt{3}}{3} \\]\n\nComparing the two areas, the maximum possible area of the equilateral triangle is:\n\n\\[ \\boxed{\\frac{100 \\sqrt{3}}{3}} \\]",
